== 4.1 Research Design

This study will employ a multi-phase mixed-methods research design, combining quantitative surveys and qualitative data to provide a comprehensive understanding of the research questions. The study is structured through three phases. To conduct the above study through a multi-phase mixed methods design with an action study as a component in one phase following these steps:

=== 1. Phase 1: Quantitative Survey

*1.1 Design:* Develop a quantitative survey questionnaire to assess teachers' attitudes towards mathematics and their implementation of problem-solving strategies. Include items that measure perceptions of problem-solving strategies, perceived effectiveness, barriers, and facilitators.

*1.2 Sampling:* Use purposive sampling to select a diverse sample of mathematics teachers who teach senior secondary mathematics. Aim to recruit a sufficient number of participants to ensure statistical power.

*1.3 Data Collection:* Administer the survey questionnaire to the selected participants either online or in person, depending on logistical considerations. Ensure that participants have adequate time to complete the survey and emphasize the importance of honest and thoughtful responses.

*1.4 Data Analysis:* Analyze the quantitative data using descriptive statistics to identify patterns and trends in teachers' attitudes towards mathematics and their implementation of problem-solving strategies. Explore differences in attitudes based on demographic variables such as teaching experience, educational background, and institutional context.

=== 2. Phase 2: Qualitative Interviews

*2.1 Design:* Develop a semi-structured interview protocol to explore teachers' experiences, perceptions, and attitudes towards mathematics and their implementation of problem-solving strategies in more depth. Include open-ended questions that allow participants to elaborate on their views and provide examples from their teaching practice.

*2.2 Sampling:* Select a subset of participants from the survey sample to participate in qualitative interviews. Aim for diversity in terms of attitudes towards mathematics and their implementation of problem-solving strategies and teaching contexts.

*2.3 Data Collection:* Conduct individual or group interviews with the selected participants to explore their perspectives on self-learning and its relevance to preparing to teach mathematics in G.C.E advanced level classes. Record the interviews and take detailed notes to ensure accuracy in data collection.

*2.4 Data Analysis:* Analyze the qualitative data using thematic analysis to identify recurring themes and patterns in teachers' narratives. Compare and contrast the qualitative findings with the quantitative results to gain a comprehensive understanding of teacher attitudes towards attitudes towards mathematics and their implementation of problem-solving strategies.

=== 3. Phase 3: Quantitative & Qualitative mixed design using experimental design

*Convenient sample*

Assess the progress of use of problem-solving strategies in mathematics after intervention.

*3.1 Design:* Integrate an experimental research component into the study to actively engage teachers in the process of attitudes towards mathematics and their implementation of problem-solving strategies and professional development. Collaborate with participating teachers to design and implement self-learning interventions aimed at enhancing their preparation for teaching mathematics in senior secondary classes.

*3.2 Implementation:* Work closely with the participating teachers to implement their problem-solving strategies interventions over a specified period. Provide support, resources, and guidance as needed to facilitate their engagement in self-directed learning activities.

*3.3 Data Collection:* Gather data on the implementation of the problems solving interventions, including teachers' experiences, challenges encountered, and perceived impact on their teaching practice. Use a combination of observation, reflection, and documentation to capture relevant information.

*3.4 Evaluation:* Evaluate the effectiveness of the attitudes towards mathematics and their implementation of problem-solving strategies interventions in enhancing teachers' preparation for teaching mathematics. Collect feedback from participating teachers and stakeholders to inform future iterations of the interventions.

=== Participants

- *Population*: Secondary school mathematics teachers in Sri Lanka.
- *Sample*: A Convenient sample of 150 teachers from various regions, ensuring representation from urban, suburban, and rural schools.

== 4.2 Data Collection

+ *Survey*: A structured questionnaire will be administered to collect quantitative data on teachers' attitudes, beliefs, and reported practices.
+ *Interviews*: Semi-structured interviews with a subset of 30 teachers to gain in-depth qualitative insights into their experiences and challenges.
+ *Classroom Observations*: Observations of 20 mathematics classes to examine the practical implementation of problem-solving strategies and triangulate data from surveys and interviews.

== 4.3 Data Analysis

+ *Quantitative Data*: Statistical analysis (e.g., correlation, regression) to identify relationships between teachers' attitudes, beliefs, and their instructional practices.
+ *Qualitative Data*: Thematic analysis of interview transcripts and observation notes to identify common themes and patterns.
